# Bare-Metal Assembly

- Write NASM-style x86-64 assembly and assume a UEFI-era, long-mode, APIC-oriented environment unless the file explicitly documents an earlier boot phase.
- Do not introduce BIOS interrupt assumptions, VGA text-mode assumptions, or new PIC-routing paths.
- Comment non-obvious register contracts, MMIO access patterns, interrupt setup, page-table manipulation, and hand-off conventions so later debugging does not depend on guesswork.
- Keep assembly minimal and explicit. Move policy and higher-level flow into C when possible.
- Match the existing bootloader and kernel calling conventions, structure layouts, and memory assumptions exactly.

## Example

```asm
; Do not rely on BIOS int 10h or VGA text mode here.
; Use the UEFI-provided framebuffer and APIC-era interrupt model instead.
```
